1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,200
[Extreme close-up: a battered silver watch on an orange sleeve. The display flips to 08:20 and starts counting down.]

2
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,260
NARRATOR: Six fifty-two a.m. Your watch starts counting down. You didn't set it.

3
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:09,360
[Pull back from the watch to a sunlit kitchen. Mira draws at the table. Biscuit stares at her toast.]

4
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:16,645
NARRATOR: Eight minutes and twenty seconds. The kettle is on. Your little sister Mira is drawing at the table, and Biscuit is staring at her toast like it owes him money.

5
00:00:16,995 --> 00:00:19,355
MIRA: Biscuit. You already had breakfast.

6
00:00:19,705 --> 00:00:21,270
NARRATOR: He disagrees.

7
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:24,970
[Through the window and out over the city in gold morning light. Traffic, birds, a perfect sky.]

8
00:00:22,070 --> 00:00:30,780
NARRATOR: Outside, it is the most ordinary morning of the year. Twenty-two degrees. Not a single cloud. Nobody out there knows what you know.

9
00:00:31,330 --> 00:00:34,220
[Back on the watch: 00:03, 00:02, 00:01. Sunlight on the glass.]

10
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:33,420
NARRATOR: Three. Two. One.

11
00:00:34,370 --> 00:00:37,520
[The light goes out like a switch. Silence. In the dark window, stars appear over the city — at breakfast.]

12
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:38,405
NARRATOR: And the Sun goes out.

13
00:00:40,155 --> 00:00:43,305
[Wide over the city: streetlights flick on automatically, people step onto balconies, every face turned up at a sky full of stars.]

14
00:00:40,405 --> 00:00:44,115
NARRATOR: No flash. No sound. Just stars... at breakfast.

15
00:00:46,065 --> 00:00:49,215
[Title: THE SUN WENT OUT.]

16
00:00:50,665 --> 00:00:53,815
[The Sun dark at frame left; a last thin shell of yellow light races across space toward a tiny Earth, which is still lit.]

17
00:00:50,915 --> 00:00:57,000
NARRATOR: Here is the part that should scare you. Sunlight takes eight minutes and twenty seconds to reach Earth.

18
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:04,710
NARRATOR: So the Sun did not go out just now. It went out eight minutes ago... at the exact second your watch started counting.

19
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:09,010
[Earth and the Moon. The light reaches the Moon a moment later — and it switches off like a bulb.]

20
00:01:06,110 --> 00:01:12,970
NARRATOR: One second later, the Moon goes dark too. It never had any light of its own. It was only ever a mirror.

21
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:16,670
[Top-down: Earth's circular orbit glows faintly. Earth leaves the curve and slides off along a straight dotted line into the dark.]

22
00:01:13,770 --> 00:01:22,355
NARRATOR: And with nothing left to pull it around, Earth stops curving. It flies off in a straight line, at thirty kilometres every second.

23
00:01:22,905 --> 00:01:26,055
[Low angle, tilted: the street below the apartment, people frozen mid-step, phones held up as torches, all staring at the stars.]

24
00:01:23,155 --> 00:01:32,065
NARRATOR: Everyone is in the street, staring up. Nobody can get a phone call through, because everyone on Earth is calling everyone else at the same time.

25
00:01:32,615 --> 00:01:35,765
[Over your shoulder at the dark window. Mira hugs Biscuit. A candle. Your reflection in the glass.]

26
00:01:32,865 --> 00:01:38,100
NARRATOR: Here is the strange thing. It is still twenty-two degrees. Nothing feels different.

27
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:44,360
NARRATOR: That's the trap. Your first problem isn't the dark. It's what the dark is about to do to the heat.

28
00:01:44,910 --> 00:01:47,360
[The watch face: DAY 001 · 9°C.]

29
00:01:47,510 --> 00:01:50,660
[Torchlight. You drag a mattress into the smallest bedroom and pin blankets over the window while Mira hands you tape.]

30
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:57,995
NARRATOR: So you pick one room. The smallest one, with the fewest windows. Every blanket you own goes over the glass, because a window leaks heat like an open door.

31
00:01:58,545 --> 00:02:01,695
[Bird’s-eye: the three of you under a mountain of blankets, a warm orange glow where your bodies are.]

32
00:01:58,795 --> 00:02:07,605
NARRATOR: A resting human gives off about a hundred watts of heat. You, your sister and a dog? That is a small heater that never switches off.

33
00:02:07,955 --> 00:02:09,665
MIRA: Biscuit is the warmest.

34
00:02:10,015 --> 00:02:12,325
NARRATOR: Biscuit is also the smelliest.

35
00:02:12,875 --> 00:02:16,025
[Bathroom by torchlight: the tub filling, frost creeping across the mirror, bottles lined up on the floor.]

36
00:02:13,125 --> 00:02:20,660
NARRATOR: Second problem: water. When pipes freeze, the ice inside them grows by nine percent, and the pipes split open.

37
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:29,720
NARRATOR: So before that happens, you fill the bathtub. Every pot. Every bottle. Two hundred litres. If you are careful, that is four weeks.

38
00:02:30,270 --> 00:02:32,520
[The watch face: DAY 003 · −6°C. Frost on the glass.]

39
00:02:32,670 --> 00:02:35,820
[Over the frozen city, green and violet curtains of aurora ripple across the stars. You and Mira on the balcony, faces lit green.]

40
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:38,255
NARRATOR: On the third night, the sky turns green. For one second, you think it is coming back.

41
00:02:38,605 --> 00:02:47,115
NARRATOR: It isn't. These are particles the Sun threw out before it died, still crossing space, still arriving. The Sun's last letter.

42
00:02:49,265 --> 00:02:52,415
[Close-up: a small finger draws a sun in the frost on the window. The aurora glows through the lines.]

43
00:02:49,515 --> 00:02:52,225
MIRA: I'm drawing it so it remembers where we live.

44
00:02:52,575 --> 00:02:55,015
NARRATOR: She will draw one every single day.

45
00:02:55,565 --> 00:02:58,715
[Close on an old radio. The dial sweeps through static, then stops on one clear station.]

46
00:02:55,815 --> 00:03:00,350
NARRATOR: That night, every radio station on Earth is static. Except one.

47
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,690
(RADIO): Go down. Go down.

48
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:07,925
NARRATOR: Two words, over and over. No name. No explanation.

49
00:03:08,475 --> 00:03:10,725
[The watch face: DAY 007 · −18°C.]

50
00:03:10,875 --> 00:03:14,025
[High wide over the snowbound city. Block by block, the lights die, a wave of darkness rolling toward the camera.]

51
00:03:11,125 --> 00:03:23,760
NARRATOR: Day seven. The average temperature on Earth is minus eighteen. Every heater in the city is running at once, and the power grid cannot take it. You watch the city go dark... one block at a time.

52
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:28,460
[You set food out on the snowy balcony like a shelf. Biscuit watches the sausages very closely.]

53
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:33,170
NARRATOR: Good news: the whole world is now a freezer. Your food will never go off. Bad news: there are eleven days of it left.

54
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:36,870
[Extreme close-up: a match flares beside a camping stove. Your eyes in the light. You blow it out.]

55
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:44,080
NARRATOR: You want to light a fire inside. Don't. In a sealed room, a flame makes carbon monoxide. You can't see it. You can't smell it. You just fall asleep.

56
00:03:44,630 --> 00:03:47,780
[The building door swings open below. Biscuit bolts past legs and out into the dark snow. You grab the torch and run after him.]

57
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:52,415
NARRATOR: Then someone opens the front door downstairs. And Biscuit bolts. Straight out, into the dark, into minus twenty.

58
00:03:52,765 --> 00:03:53,875
MIRA: Biscuit!

59
00:03:54,225 --> 00:03:56,960
NARRATOR: You grab the torch, and you go after him...

60
00:03:57,510 --> 00:04:00,660
[Your torch beam sweeps across buried cars. Breath freezing in the air. Then, in the snow: paw prints.]

61
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:07,395
NARRATOR: Your breath freezes in the air. No cars. No birds. No wind. Just silence. Then, footprints in the snow. Small ones. Four legs.

62
00:04:07,945 --> 00:04:11,095
[The prints lead to a metro entrance. Warm air rises from the stairs as steam. Biscuit sits on the top step, tail going.]

63
00:04:08,195 --> 00:04:17,030
NARRATOR: They lead to the metro station. And out of the stairs... warm air is rising. Biscuit found the one thing nobody else was looking for.

64
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:21,530
[The camera cranes straight down through snow, soil and rock in cross-section; colours shift from icy blue to warm orange as it descends.]

65
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:31,965
NARRATOR: Underground, the ground still remembers last summer. And deeper still, the rock gets warmer, about twenty-five degrees for every kilometre. Earth has its own heat. It never needed the Sun for that.

66
00:04:32,515 --> 00:04:35,665
[You carry Mira on your back down the metro stairs into the amber light, Biscuit leading the way.]

67
00:04:32,765 --> 00:04:39,125
NARRATOR: The radio said go down. So you go down. And you start to wonder how the voice on that radio knew.

68
00:04:39,675 --> 00:04:41,925
[The watch face: DAY 030 · −40°C.]

69
00:04:42,075 --> 00:04:45,225
[A crane shot over the platform: tents, strings of lights, train carriages turned into homes, children playing, a hundred lanterns.]

70
00:04:42,325 --> 00:04:53,135
NARRATOR: Day thirty. Ten thousand people live in the tunnels now. Train carriages are bedrooms. The platforms are streets. Someone has hung up lights shaped like stars.

71
00:04:53,685 --> 00:04:56,835
[Biscuit sits in the middle of a ring of children, every one of them holding out a snack. He looks extremely pleased with himself.]

72
00:04:53,935 --> 00:05:02,595
NARRATOR: Biscuit is now the most popular resident of the underground. He has eleven new best friends, and every single one of them has snacks.

73
00:05:03,145 --> 00:05:06,295
[Seven a.m. underground: every lamp on the platform swings up to full brightness at once, warm as a sunrise. People stretch, kids cheer.]

74
00:05:03,395 --> 00:05:14,230
NARRATOR: Without the Sun, your body forgets what time it is. So at seven every morning, someone turns every lamp up to full, and the whole tunnel pretends it is sunrise.

75
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:17,930
[A cross-section of the harbour: white ice spreading across the surface, dark liquid ocean below, fish still moving.]

76
00:05:15,030 --> 00:05:25,490
NARRATOR: Above you, the sea is freezing from the top down. But ice floats, so it works like a lid. Under it, the ocean stays liquid, for a very, very long time.

77
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:29,190
[Mira, wrapped in your parka, looks up at you. You crouch to her level.]

78
00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:28,025
MIRA: Are we going to run out of air?

79
00:05:28,375 --> 00:05:39,310
NARRATOR: No. The plants stopped making oxygen on day one. But the air already holds enough for every human alive to breathe for thousands of years. Air was never the clock.

80
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:43,010
[A long ration line under a ticket hall glowing magenta with grow lights. Rows of seedlings.]

81
00:05:40,110 --> 00:05:51,520
NARRATOR: Food is. Two thousand calories, per person, per day. The tunnels have ninety days of it. And a ticket hall full of grow lights, running on the one power plant still alive.

82
00:05:52,070 --> 00:05:54,320
[The watch face: DAY 100 · −51°C.]

83
00:05:54,470 --> 00:05:57,620
[Powers-of-ten pull-back: the tunnel, the city, the dark Earth, then the whole solar system. Earth’s straight path has carried it beyond the orbit of Mars.]

84
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:02,430
NARRATOR: Day one hundred. Earth has now drifted farther out than Mars. No human being has ever been this far from the Sun.

85
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:06,930
[The grow lights flicker and die. Then the string lights. Lanterns only. Breath clouds. Ten thousand people go silent.]

86
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:08,490
NARRATOR: Then the lights go out. All of them. The grow lights. The heaters. The pumps.

87
00:06:08,840 --> 00:06:18,530
NARRATOR: In the dark, you hear ten thousand people stop talking at the same time. The tunnels start losing a degree every day... and there is nowhere left to go.

88
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:22,230
[The lights stutter back on. People cheer, hats in the air. You lift Mira off the ground.]

89
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:30,265
NARRATOR: Eleven days later, the engineers bring the plant back. People cheer like it is New Year. By day one hundred and eighty, there are a hundred and eighty suns on the tunnel wall.

90
00:06:30,815 --> 00:06:33,965
[Deep under the ice, black smokers pour heat into the dark ocean. Tube worms, white crabs, drifting bacteria glow faintly.]

91
00:06:31,065 --> 00:06:47,550
NARRATOR: And on the radio, there is good news. Four kilometres under the ice, at the bottom of the ocean, there are cracks where the Earth breathes out heat. And around them, life. Worms, crabs, bacteria. Life that never needed the Sun. Not for a single day.

92
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:52,250
[The radio again, in the tunnel. Under the news, the old voice is still there — then it changes.]

93
00:06:49,350 --> 00:06:55,760
NARRATOR: Underneath the broadcast, quieter, the other voice is still there. But tonight, it says something new.

94
00:06:56,110 --> 00:06:57,275
(RADIO): Look up.

95
00:06:58,425 --> 00:07:00,875
[The watch face: DAY 365 · −73°C.]

96
00:07:01,025 --> 00:07:04,175
[A long dolly along the tunnel wall: hundreds of chalk suns, each a little different. Mira draws the last one.]

97
00:07:01,275 --> 00:07:07,585
NARRATOR: Day three hundred and sixty-five. One year. Three hundred and sixty-five suns on the wall.

98
00:07:09,335 --> 00:07:12,485
[Low angle: you climb the frozen metro stairs toward the surface, torch in hand, frost on your hood.]

99
00:07:09,585 --> 00:07:17,395
NARRATOR: You climb up to the surface. Minus seventy-three degrees. Your eyelashes freeze with a single breath. And then you look up.

100
00:07:18,545 --> 00:07:21,695
[You, tiny, on a buried street. Above you, the Milky Way blazes from horizon to horizon — brighter than any human has ever seen it.]

101
00:07:18,795 --> 00:07:25,655
NARRATOR: No Sun. No city lights. No haze. Just the most perfect sky any human being has ever seen.

102
00:07:28,405 --> 00:07:31,555
[The solar system map again: Earth’s straight path now runs past the orbit of Jupiter.]

103
00:07:28,655 --> 00:07:34,690
NARRATOR: Earth is out past the orbit of Jupiter now, carrying everyone you have ever met into the dark.

104
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:39,190
[A perfectly round black disc slides across the stars, swallowing them as it moves. The Moon, invisible except for where it is not.]

105
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:44,050
NARRATOR: And the Moon is still with you. You can't see it. But you can see where it isn't. A perfect black circle, eating the stars.

106
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:48,950
[The watch beeps. 08:20 again, counting down.]

107
00:07:46,050 --> 00:07:49,560
NARRATOR: Then your watch beeps. Eight minutes and twenty seconds.

108
00:07:50,910 --> 00:07:52,360
[You blink. White.]

109
00:07:52,510 --> 00:07:55,660
[The same sunlit kitchen as the opening, same framing. Mira draws. Biscuit stares at the toast. You stand frozen in your parka.]

110
00:07:52,760 --> 00:08:00,645
NARRATOR: Six fifty-two a.m. The kettle is on. Sunlight on the table. Mira is drawing. Biscuit is staring at her toast.

111
00:08:00,995 --> 00:08:03,835
NARRATOR: Nobody remembers. Nobody but you.

112
00:08:04,385 --> 00:08:07,535
[Close on the window in summer sunlight. In the corner, a patch of frost in the shape of a child’s sun, slowly melting.]

113
00:08:04,635 --> 00:08:09,870
NARRATOR: Except, on the window, in the middle of summer... there is frost. In the shape of a sun.

114
00:08:11,620 --> 00:08:14,770
[The watch: 71:59:59 and counting.]

115
00:08:11,870 --> 00:08:18,780
NARRATOR: And your watch is counting again. Seventy-one hours. Fifty-nine minutes. This time... it isn't the Sun.

116
00:08:19,930 --> 00:08:23,080
[Slow push in on you at the sunny window, Biscuit leaning against your leg, Mira looking up at you.]

117
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:26,790
NARRATOR: So. If your watch started counting down right now... what's the first thing you would do? Tell us in the comments.

118
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:31,125
NARRATOR: And subscribe. Because next time, the countdown is shorter.

119
00:08:32,475 --> 00:08:35,625
[End screen: the watch ticking at the centre, space for two video cards.]
